      "paperId": "SCOPUS-PUBLICATION:85083943620",
      "doi": "",
      "title": "Modeling and control of a steerable cruciform parachute system through experimental testing",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Herrington S.M."
        },
        {
          "name": "Renzelman J.T."
        },
        {
          "name": "Fields T.D."
        },
        {
          "name": "Yakimenko O.A."
        }
      ],
      "year": 2019,
      "abstract": "This paper presents an experimental approach for testing a steerable cruciform parachute system using a vertical wind tunnel. A controller to stabilize the canopy heading was developed and tuned. A model which captures the closed-loop heading dynamics was identified and a rudimentary estimate of glide ratio (GR) was obtained using inverse dynamics. The heading stabilization controller developed during the vertical wind tunnel experiments was applicable during outdoor flight testing with only minor modifications, and the closed-loop heading model and GR model both produce results which are consistent with data collected during outdoor flight testing. The vertical wind tunnel testing methodology itself is discussed in terms of the cost and time savings compared to traditional outdoor flight testing. Additionally, the extensibility of vertical wind tunnel testing methodology to other parachute systems is discussed. The vertical wind tunnel approach to testing a system like the steerable cruciform provides the capability to investigate the viability of an untested parachute system rapidly while still providing realistic performance, thereby creating a repeatable and inexpensive alternative to outdoor flight testing.",
